--- IR --->
# the unoptimized module on purpose: `-O` inlines this constructor into its one caller, and a claim about
# a function that no longer exists is a check that quietly stops checking. what is pinned here is the
# *choice*, which is made before any optimizer runs

# the copy constructor itself is now written through `extend`, so what it contains is two calls and no
# branch at all - the `const if` it used to hold is `extend`'s, checked below
CHECK: define linkonce_odr %"array<int32>" @_arrayZZMRBCLC5arrayIMLPiEGMLPi(
CHECK-NOT: br i1
CHECK: call void @_M5array_reserve_exact
CHECK: call void @_M5array_extend

CHECK: define linkonce_odr void @_M5array_extendZZMRBMLC5arrayIMLPiEZMRBCLC5arrayIMLPiEGMLPi(

# **the branch is not folded here, it is gone.** `const if (mem::is_trivially_copyable<T>())` is decided in
# the *tree*: AST::ConstFolding replaced the statement with the arm the condition selected, so there is no
# second arm and nothing for an optimizer to see through.
#
# this used to check `br i1 true`, which was the weaker claim - it said the condition had folded while the
# element loop's blocks were still materialized behind it, removed by LLVM under `-O` and shipped at `-O0`.
# an absence is the stronger assertion, and it is the one the feature is about.
#
# the `$n == 0` guard's own `br i1` is a real runtime branch and is deliberately not what this forbids
CHECK-NOT: br i1 true
CHECK-NOT: br i1 false

# nothing here reaches the element operator, which is what the loop arm is built out of. it is not merely
# unreachable now - it is absent, and so is the `operator []` instantiation the arm used to create
CHECK-NOT: @_operatorx20x5b

# and the arm it selected is the one call this whole fast path is about
CHECK: call void @_mem_copy

# **the mirror, in truncate().** the same source branches on `mem::needs_destruction<T>()`, and for an
# int32 that arm is what allocated `%doomed` and called `mem::take`. the whole body is now the one store
# `count()` promises.
#
# checked here rather than in `clear()` because this is where the teardown lives now - `clear()` is
# `truncate(0)`, the way `push` is the append and `at` is the element operator. the *only* `br i1` left is
# the real `$count >= $this->len` guard, so unlike the constructor above this cannot assert on branches
CHECK: define linkonce_odr void @_M5array_truncateZZMRBMLC5arrayIMLPiEZMLPyGMLPi(
CHECK-NOT: alloca i32
CHECK-NOT: call
CHECK: ret void
